# Flutter/Dart patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' design guidance to Flutter's widget lifecycle, state handling and async patterns. The rules apply whichever of `Provider`, `Riverpod`, `Bloc` or plain `StatefulWidget` the project uses; match the one already in use, do not introduce a second.

## Widget lifecycle correctness

- `initState()` is for one-time setup that needs `this` (subscribing to a
  controller, starting a timer); it calls `super.initState()` first. Read an
  inherited widget in `didChangeDependencies()`, not `initState()`.
- `dispose()` releases everything acquired: cancel every `StreamSubscription`,
  call `.dispose()` on every `TextEditingController`/`AnimationController`/
  `FocusNode`/`ScrollController` the `State` created, and call `super.dispose()` last.
- React to a changed constructor parameter in `didUpdateWidget(oldWidget)`
  (`oldWidget.someId != widget.someId`), not in `build()`.

## setState and BuildContext after an async gap

- After any `await` in a `State` method, check `mounted` before `setState()`.
- After any `await` in a callback holding a `BuildContext` (`onPressed`, a
  `Future` continuation), check `context.mounted` before using it, whether the
  context came from a `State` or a `StatelessWidget`'s `build` parameter.
- The fix is an early-return `if` on `context.mounted`/`mounted` immediately
  after the `await`, not a `try`/`catch` around the error and not skipping the
  check because the call "usually" resolves quickly.

## Async patterns

- Prefer `async`/`await` over chained `.then()` beyond a single continuation.
- For a value arriving over time, use a `Stream` with `StreamBuilder` (or the
  state-management approach's own listener), not a `Future` polled on a timer.
- Cancel a manually subscribed `StreamSubscription` in `dispose()`;
  `StreamBuilder` manages its own.
- Guard `snapshot.connectionState` in a `FutureBuilder`/`StreamBuilder` before
  reading `snapshot.data`.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A `TextEditingController`/`AnimationController`/`StreamSubscription`/`FocusNode`
  created in `initState` with no matching release in `dispose()`.
- `BuildContext` or `setState()` used after an `await` with no `context.mounted`/
  `mounted` check immediately before.
- Business logic (network calls, parsing, validation) inside `build()` instead of
  a controller/view-model/state object.
- Mixing state-management approaches in one feature (a `Riverpod` provider read
  via `ref` next to an ad hoc `InheritedWidget`/`setState` for the same state).
